// Package-level bump allocator over one qualified `Extent`
// (wiki/spec/resources/allocation.md). The compiler owns no allocator
// semantics here: it only checks that the package conserves the `Granted`
// custody it was handed, through `Granted::content`, `old`, and `separate`.
//
// Every extent the allocator touches is `Extent in Granted & Vacant`:
// granted authority over a range carrying no live resident. `Vacant` is the
// dormant-content state of the placed-access contract
// (wiki/spec/resources/placed_access.md), and ordinary extent split/merge
// reject resident content, so `merge` and `reset` demanding `Vacant` parts
// are what enforce cleanup-before-return: an issued extent still holding a
// `Resident<P, T>` does not satisfy the returned-region parameters, and a
// container must retire its residents to `Vacant` before release or reset.
// Establishing the resident still waits on the undeclared place/take/read
// operations; this pins the dormant-custody shape those operations must
// satisfy.
//
// Beside the tail extent the strategy carries `remaining`, a counted byte
// residual — the contract keeps it a distinct premise from the tail's
// placement authority (wiki/spec/resources/allocation.md: "A counted byte
// residual summarizes capacity; the tail extent supplies placement. These
// are distinct premises."). The infallible request bounds the caller's
// demand on that count: `allocate` requires `length <= strategy.remaining`,
// the exact capacity requirement.
//
// Carrying the count is necessary, not cosmetic:
// - `split`'s conservation law fixes only the union of the two parts'
//   content and never `result.taken.length`, so the residual left after an
//   allocation is not derivable through `separate` and cannot be discharged
//   against `tail.length`.
// - `allocate` publishes the decrement as `result.strategy.remaining ==
//   strategy.remaining - length`. Exit checking proves that nested field's
//   exact computation, and `check_remaining` consumes the relation from the
//   exact first and second allocations. The second request consumes that
//   returned residual: its bound follows from the decrement and `48 <= capacity`.
//   Scalar and content facts remain separate proof obligations.
// - Independent scalar entry bounds survive consumption of the backing:
//   `check_remaining` proves `16 <= capacity` after the first request. Each
//   arithmetic obligation substitutes only the parameters it uses, so the
//   sibling result-field argument does not block that bound. Both `exercise`
//   and `exercise_fallible` then use `issued_first.strategy.remaining`, not
//   a caller-invented floor, for the second allocation.
// `reset` restores the residual from the recomposed range's own geometry.
//
// Shape decisions a next reader needs:
//
// - `Granted` cannot be minted from geometry, and a checked callable may
//   retain content-bearing custody only from a consumed owned input. The
//   split therefore consumes the whole tail; a `&mut` carve was rejected
//   because its result custody would come from a borrowed parameter.
//   Partitioning is a boundary requirement: the package authors the exact
//   `separate` law, and the selected provider is what establishes the two
//   qualified sub-ranges. No ordinary machine here constructs `Extent`
//   geometry.
// - Recomposition takes one `Split` record rather than two `Extent`
//   parameters: a boundary returning `Granted` custody from two consumed
//   qualified inputs is rejected as ambiguous, and the checker's exact
//   correspondence recognizes only a one-parameter identity, not `separate`.
//   An ordinary machine faces no such limit: `reset` consumes `lower` and
//   `upper` as two separate qualified parameters, and `close` recomposes
//   one buffer with the tail the same way.
// - Owned record fields carry their exact qualifications through checked
//   calls, construction and moves. `allocate` consumes the split fields
//   directly; the remaining named locals organize the exercise rather than
//   assert authority. A typed initializer must prove its source qualification.
//   The split/merge invocation is joined to its actual consumed claims and
//   returned claim identities under the authored conservation theorem.
// - An ordinary machine cannot restate the boundary's `separate` law as
//   its own `ensures`, so `allocate` and `reset` keep the exact
//   conservation inside their bodies instead of publishing it.
// - Returning an allocation does not rewind the tail. A returned region
//   stays a separate qualified value until reset, which consumes every
//   returned region together with the tail and recomposes the original
//   range. Because `Extent` is linear, a still-live allocation cannot be
//   dropped to reach reset; a partial reset has no spelling.
//
// Discoveries from the fallible-request and container slices, which bound
// what a `Vec<T>`-style owner over this chain can currently say:
//
// - `try_allocate` returns an ordinary `Attempt`: `Allocated(Issued)` or
//   `Exhausted(Bump)`. `exercise_fallible` consumes both selected payloads.
//   Their qualifications retain exact case/field paths; selecting a payload
//   requires the current tag, not just the sum's declared alternatives.
//   Rejection returns the original strategy unchanged. Recursive retired
//   lists and outcome-specific partition frontiers remain separate work.
// - Post-reset reuse remains fallible. `reset` returns its recomposed range's
//   runtime length without a guarantee connecting it to the original capacity;
//   `try_allocate` checks demand against that returned residual.
// - `BumpVec` retains one outgrown buffer in `RetiredSlot`. Growth allocates
//   from the remaining tail and preserves the old buffer separately; it does
//   not rewind allocation. Full-slot and exhausted-tail rejection both return
//   the original custody. Close merges the current buffer and tail, then
//   consumes either the empty slot or its exact retained buffer.
// - This finite slot exercises conditional returned custody, not general
//   growable storage. An unbounded retained list needs explicit placed-storage
//   indirection and a ranking proof over that representation. A recursively
//   owned inline record would have infinite layout; Omega does not box it.
//
// Growth still carries no elements. Content-preserving movement waits on the
// placed element operations, and merge still demands `Vacant` on every part
// (`tests/omega/fail/memory/bump_allocator_grow_with_live_resident`).
//
// Resident custody over an issued extent (`exercise_resident`): one
// establishment of an owned `Slot` into a `Vacant` allocation yielding
// `Extent in Granted & Resident<SlotPlacement, Slot>`, one read view, one
// owned retirement back to `Granted & Vacant`, then recomposition with the
// tail. The establishment crosses the package boundary `ResidentStorage`,
// the provider-issuance route of placed_access.md ("`ResidentContentTransfer
// <P, T>` introduces it only at an exact installed/provider-issuance
// occurrence"), spelled here as one concrete application. The compiler-owned
// `Initialize` family (encode an owned `T` into exclusive `Vacant` Stable
// storage under `P`'s evaluated plan) is not declared, so this pins the
// custody shape the placed operations must satisfy, not the encoding:
// - Recomposition refuses a live resident: constructing a `Split` field
//   from `Occupied.storage` drops `Resident<SlotPlacement, Slot>`, which the
//   weakening check rejects, so `reset`/`close` cannot fold an occupied
//   allocation back (`tests/omega/fail/memory/bump_allocator_reset_with_live_resident`).
// - A resident cannot be dropped: `Occupied.storage` is linear qualified
//   custody, so only `retire` disposes of it
//   (`tests/omega/fail/memory/bump_allocator_resident_dropped`).
// - An occupied slot cannot be placed again: `place` requires `Vacant`,
//   which an occupied storage does not carry
//   (`tests/omega/fail/memory/bump_allocator_place_into_occupied`).
//
// Discovered edges that fix the spelling:
// - Indexed membership is supported in both types and proof facts. `place`
//   keeps the `Occupied` record so the resident's ownership is explicit at
//   the package boundary, alongside the `Split` and `Retired` records.
// - Restating `placed.storage` into a typed local
//   `Extent in Granted & Resident<SlotPlacement, Slot>` carries the
//   existing instance identity from the source field, so the
//   local proves the indexed membership at a call `requires`
//   exactly as a `Vacant` restatement does (`exercise_resident_restated`:
//   `view(&resident)`, `retire_view(resident)`). The record-carried shape
//   (`exercise_resident`: `read(&Occupied)`, `retire(Occupied)`) remains
//   the form that needs no restatement at all.
// - A generic `boundary trait ResidentContentTransfer<P, T>` with an
//   `Occupied<P, T>` result parses and types, so lifting this concrete
//   route into the core schema is mechanical once its signature and receipt
//   semantics are settled.
use omega::language::core::extent;
use omega::language::core::content;

// Exact partition of one qualified vacant range into two adjacent qualified
// vacant ranges.
data Split {
    taken: Extent in Granted & Vacant;
    rest: Extent in Granted & Vacant;
}

boundary trait ExtentPartition {
    machine split(whole: Extent in Granted & Vacant, length: u64) -> Split
    ensures
        Granted::content(old(&whole))
        == separate(
            Granted::content(&result.taken),
            Granted::content(&result.rest),
        );

    machine merge(parts: Split) -> Extent
    ensures
        result in Granted & Vacant,
        separate(
            Granted::content(old(&parts.taken)),
            Granted::content(old(&parts.rest)),
        )
        == Granted::content(&result);
}

// Bump policy: the allocation is the low end of the tail; the rest is the new
// tail. Nothing about the allocation's later cleanup moves the tail.
// `remaining` is the counted byte residual summarizing the tail's capacity;
// it is a separate premise from the tail's placement authority.
data Bump {
    tail: Extent in Granted & Vacant;
    remaining: u64;
}

data Issued {
    strategy: Bump;
    allocation: Extent in Granted & Vacant;
}

data Attempt {
    case Allocated(issued: Issued);
    case Exhausted(strategy: Bump);
}

// Failure returns the unchanged strategy; success returns its exact partition.
machine try_allocate(partition: &ExtentPartition, strategy: Bump, length: u64) -> Attempt
reaches ExtentPartition
{
    transition length <= strategy.remaining {
        true -> Attempt::Allocated { issued: allocate(partition, strategy, length) }
        _ -> Attempt::Exhausted { strategy: strategy }
    }
}

// Infallible request: the caller proves the exact capacity requirement
// `length <= strategy.remaining` (allocation.md), and the body decrements
// the residual by the same length the requires bounds — the requires
// ordering is what licenses the subtraction.
machine allocate(partition: &ExtentPartition, strategy: Bump, length: u64) -> Issued
requires
    length <= strategy.remaining
ensures
    result.strategy.remaining == strategy.remaining - length
reaches ExtentPartition
{
    let tail: Extent in Granted & Vacant = strategy.tail;
    let parts: Split = partition.split(tail, length);
    Issued {
        strategy: Bump {
            tail: parts.rest,
            remaining: strategy.remaining - length,
        },
        allocation: parts.taken,
    }
}

// Exercise the published count at an ordinary caller obligation, independently
// of the extent's custody. This check must use the exact allocation invocation;
// the source constructor or a second allocation cannot stand in for its result.
machine check_remaining(remaining: u64, previous: u64, length: u64)
requires
    length <= previous;
    remaining == previous - length
{}

// Reset after full return. `lower` was issued before `upper`, so the original
// range is `lower ++ upper ++ tail`. Both returned regions are consumed, and
// both must still be vacant: a region whose resident has not been retired
// does not type as `Vacant` and cannot reach recomposition.
machine reset(
    partition: &ExtentPartition,
    strategy: Bump,
    lower: Extent in Granted & Vacant,
    upper: Extent in Granted & Vacant
) -> Bump
reaches ExtentPartition
{
    let tail: Extent in Granted & Vacant = strategy.tail;
    let upper_and_tail: Extent in Granted & Vacant = partition.merge(Split {
        taken: upper,
        rest: tail,
    });
    let whole: Extent in Granted & Vacant = partition.merge(Split {
        taken: lower,
        rest: upper_and_tail,
    });
    let restored_residual: u64 = whole.length;
    Bump { tail: whole, remaining: restored_residual }
}

// The reservation half of a `Vec<T>`-style owner over the bump chain: one
// live buffer, the strategy it was issued from, and one retained old buffer.
// `buffer.length` is the capacity measure. Elements and general retained
// storage still need the placed-access route described in the header.
data BumpVec {
    strategy: Bump;
    buffer: Extent in Granted & Vacant;
    retained_buffer: RetiredSlot;
}

// Open a reservation of `capacity` bytes. `residual` is the caller's stated
// capacity premise for the backing, tied to its geometry by the second
// requires clause. Later allocations consume `allocate`'s returned residual.
machine open(
    partition: &ExtentPartition,
    whole: Extent in Granted & Vacant,
    capacity: u64,
    residual: u64
) -> BumpVec
requires
    capacity <= residual;
    residual == whole.length
reaches ExtentPartition
{
    let issued: Issued = allocate(
        partition,
        Bump { tail: whole, remaining: residual },
        capacity,
    );
    let strategy: Bump = issued.strategy;
    let remaining: u64 = strategy.remaining;
    let tail: Extent in Granted & Vacant = strategy.tail;
    let buffer: Extent in Granted & Vacant = issued.allocation;
    BumpVec {
        strategy: Bump { tail: tail, remaining: remaining },
        buffer: buffer,
        retained_buffer: RetiredSlot::Empty,
    }
}

// The recomposition receipt: one qualified extent standing for the range
// `close` folded back. A record, not a bare extent, because the caller-side
// claim mapping is only published by a construction transfer — a bare
// `whole`/`as` return leaves the caller's bound result without a conserved
// claim mapping (the same reason `reset` returns `Bump`).
data Recomposed {
    backing: Extent in Granted & Vacant;
}

// Close the container: recompose current buffer, tail, and retained buffer.
// The buffer
// must still be `Vacant` — closing a container whose elements were never
// retired does not type. Both slot cases must return the entire held backing.
machine close(partition: &ExtentPartition, vec: BumpVec) -> Recomposed
reaches ExtentPartition
{
    let strategy: Bump = vec.strategy;
    let tail: Extent in Granted & Vacant = strategy.tail;
    let buffer: Extent in Granted & Vacant = vec.buffer;
    let whole: Extent in Granted & Vacant = partition.merge(Split {
        taken: buffer,
        rest: tail,
    });
    let remaining: u64 = whole.length;
    let restored: Bump = reset_retired(
        partition,
        Bump { tail: whole, remaining: remaining },
        vec.retained_buffer,
    );
    Recomposed { backing: restored.tail }
}

// Reserve a new buffer from the tail without returning the old buffer to it.
// The finite retained slot makes a second growth reject without losing any
// claim. Capacity exhaustion likewise preserves the existing container.
data Growth {
    case Grown(vec: BumpVec);
    case Rejected(vec: BumpVec);
}

machine grow(partition: &ExtentPartition, vec: BumpVec, capacity: u64) -> Growth
reaches ExtentPartition
{
    let strategy: Bump = vec.strategy;
    let buffer: Extent in Granted & Vacant = vec.buffer;
    transition vec.retained_buffer {
        RetiredSlot::Empty -> reserve(partition, strategy, buffer, capacity)
        RetiredSlot::Held { storage } -> Growth::Rejected {
            vec: BumpVec {
                strategy: strategy,
                buffer: buffer,
                retained_buffer: RetiredSlot::Held { storage: storage },
            },
        }
    }

    state reserve(
        partition: &ExtentPartition,
        strategy: Bump,
        buffer: Extent in Granted & Vacant,
        capacity: u64
    ) -> Growth {
        let attempt: Attempt = try_allocate(partition, strategy, capacity);
        transition attempt {
            Attempt::Allocated { issued } -> Growth::Grown {
                vec: BumpVec {
                    strategy: issued.strategy,
                    buffer: issued.allocation,
                    retained_buffer: RetiredSlot::Held { storage: buffer },
                },
            }
            Attempt::Exhausted { strategy } -> Growth::Rejected {
                vec: BumpVec {
                    strategy: strategy,
                    buffer: buffer,
                    retained_buffer: RetiredSlot::Empty,
                },
            }
        }
    }
}

// Two coexisting allocations, cleanup of each, then reset to the whole range,
// chained through the package machines `allocate` and `reset`. The caller
// proves the exact capacity requirement at the entry — `capacity` covers the
// whole 16 + 32 plan and equals the backing's geometry — then threads the
// actual returned residual into the second request. Each returned record's
// qualified fields are restated
// through exactly typed locals in the custody order described in the header.
machine exercise(
    partition: &ExtentPartition,
    whole: Extent in Granted & Vacant,
    capacity: u64
) -> Extent
requires
    48 <= capacity;
    capacity == whole.length
reaches ExtentPartition
{
    let issued_first: Issued = allocate(
        partition,
        Bump { tail: whole, remaining: capacity },
        16,
    );
    let first_tail: Extent in Granted & Vacant = issued_first.strategy.tail;
    check_remaining(issued_first.strategy.remaining, capacity, 16);
    let issued_second: Issued = allocate(
        partition,
        Bump { tail: first_tail, remaining: issued_first.strategy.remaining },
        32,
    );
    check_remaining(issued_second.strategy.remaining, issued_first.strategy.remaining, 32);
    let second_residual: u64 = issued_second.strategy.remaining;
    let second_tail: Extent in Granted & Vacant = issued_second.strategy.tail;
    let second_allocation: Extent in Granted & Vacant = issued_second.allocation;
    let first_allocation: Extent in Granted & Vacant = issued_first.allocation;
    let restored: Bump = reset(
        partition,
        Bump { tail: second_tail, remaining: second_residual },
        first_allocation,
        second_allocation,
    );
    restored.tail as Extent
}

// A second allocation epoch uses the real fallible request. `demand` is
// unrelated to the original capacity, so both returned outcomes must work:
// success recomposes its allocation and tail; rejection returns the strategy's
// unchanged tail. No caller-side guard substitutes for the returned custody.
machine exercise_fallible(
    partition: &ExtentPartition,
    whole: Extent in Granted & Vacant,
    capacity: u64,
    demand: u64
) -> Extent
requires
    48 <= capacity;
    capacity == whole.length
reaches ExtentPartition
{
    let issued_first: Issued = allocate(
        partition,
        Bump { tail: whole, remaining: capacity },
        16,
    );
    let first_tail: Extent in Granted & Vacant = issued_first.strategy.tail;
    let issued_second: Issued = allocate(
        partition,
        Bump { tail: first_tail, remaining: issued_first.strategy.remaining },
        32,
    );
    let second_residual: u64 = issued_second.strategy.remaining;
    let second_tail: Extent in Granted & Vacant = issued_second.strategy.tail;
    let second_allocation: Extent in Granted & Vacant = issued_second.allocation;
    let first_allocation: Extent in Granted & Vacant = issued_first.allocation;
    let restored: Bump = reset(
        partition,
        Bump { tail: second_tail, remaining: second_residual },
        first_allocation,
        second_allocation,
    );
    let attempt: Attempt = try_allocate(partition, restored, demand);
    transition attempt {
        Attempt::Allocated { issued } -> reuse(partition, issued)
        Attempt::Exhausted { strategy } -> rejected(strategy)
    }

    // Allocation already happened inside the request. This arm returns both
    // selected claims to the partition boundary for exact recomposition.
    state reuse(
        partition: &ExtentPartition,
        issued: Issued
    ) -> Extent
    {
        let strategy: Bump = issued.strategy;
        let new_tail: Extent in Granted & Vacant = strategy.tail;
        let allocation: Extent in Granted & Vacant = issued.allocation;
        let recomposed: Extent in Granted & Vacant = partition.merge(Split {
            taken: allocation,
            rest: new_tail,
        });
        recomposed as Extent
    }

    // The rejected arm returns the recomposed backing untouched — the
    // request never happened, so the caller's custody is unchanged.
    state rejected(strategy: Bump) -> Extent {
        strategy.tail as Extent
    }
}

// The reservation container over the chain: open a 16-byte buffer, then
// close — the live buffer and the tail recompose into the backing's suffix.
// The record fields retain their existing qualifications while this exercise
// explicitly rebuilds the container before returning its backing.
machine exercise_vec(
    partition: &ExtentPartition,
    whole: Extent in Granted & Vacant,
    capacity: u64
) -> Extent
requires
    48 <= capacity;
    capacity == whole.length
reaches ExtentPartition
{
    let vec: BumpVec = open(partition, whole, 16, capacity);
    let strategy: Bump = vec.strategy;
    let remaining: u64 = strategy.remaining;
    let tail: Extent in Granted & Vacant = strategy.tail;
    let buffer: Extent in Granted & Vacant = vec.buffer;
    let recomposed: Recomposed = close(
        partition,
        BumpVec {
            strategy: Bump { tail: tail, remaining: remaining },
            buffer: buffer,
            retained_buffer: vec.retained_buffer,
        },
    );
    let backing: Extent in Granted & Vacant = recomposed.backing;
    backing as Extent
}

// Reserve 16 bytes, request 32 more while retaining the old buffer, then
// request another growth and close either returned outcome. The second
// request exercises full-slot rejection without dropping retained custody.
machine exercise_grow(
    partition: &ExtentPartition,
    whole: Extent in Granted & Vacant,
    capacity: u64
) -> Extent
requires
    48 <= capacity;
    capacity == whole.length
reaches ExtentPartition
{
    let vec: BumpVec = open(partition, whole, 16, capacity);
    let strategy: Bump = vec.strategy;
    let remaining: u64 = strategy.remaining;
    let tail: Extent in Granted & Vacant = strategy.tail;
    let buffer: Extent in Granted & Vacant = vec.buffer;
    let growth: Growth = grow(
        partition,
        BumpVec {
            strategy: Bump { tail: tail, remaining: remaining },
            buffer: buffer,
            retained_buffer: vec.retained_buffer,
        },
        32,
    );
    transition growth {
        Growth::Grown { vec } -> retry(partition, vec)
        Growth::Rejected { vec } -> finish(partition, vec)
    }

    state retry(partition: &ExtentPartition, vec: BumpVec) -> Extent {
        let second: Growth = grow(partition, vec, 1);
        transition second {
            Growth::Grown { vec } -> finish(partition, vec)
            Growth::Rejected { vec } -> finish(partition, vec)
        }
    }

    state finish(partition: &ExtentPartition, vec: BumpVec) -> Extent {
        let recomposed: Recomposed = close(partition, vec);
        recomposed.backing as Extent
    }
}

// The same two-allocation plan spelled directly over the partition boundary,
// kept as the control surface for restating `Split` fields from a boundary
// result.
machine exercise_boundary(
    partition: &ExtentPartition,
    whole: Extent in Granted & Vacant
) -> Extent
reaches ExtentPartition
{
    let first: Split = partition.split(whole, 16);
    let first_allocation: Extent in Granted & Vacant = first.taken;
    let after_first: Extent in Granted & Vacant = first.rest;
    let second: Split = partition.split(after_first, 32);
    let second_allocation: Extent in Granted & Vacant = second.taken;
    let tail: Extent in Granted & Vacant = second.rest;
    let upper_and_tail: Extent in Granted & Vacant = partition.merge(Split {
        taken: second_allocation,
        rest: tail,
    });
    let restored: Extent in Granted & Vacant = partition.merge(Split {
        taken: first_allocation,
        rest: upper_and_tail,
    });
    restored as Extent
}

// Element type and the placement-policy index it is resident under. The
// index is identity only: no plan is evaluated for it here.
data Slot [copy] {
    value: u32;
}

data SlotPlacement {}

// Established custody: the allocation now carries one complete live `Slot`.
// The record, not a bare extent, is what crosses machines (see the header's
// restatement edge).
data Occupied {
    storage: Extent in Granted & Resident<SlotPlacement, Slot>;
}

// Owned retirement: the storage returns to `Vacant` and the value moves out.
data Retired {
    storage: Extent in Granted & Vacant;
    value: Slot;
}

// The provider-issuance route for one concrete `(SlotPlacement, Slot)`
// application. `place` and `retire` conserve the extent's content; `read`
// is a borrowed view returning a copy of the resident value.
boundary trait ResidentStorage {
    machine place(storage: Extent in Granted & Vacant, value: Slot) -> Occupied
    ensures
        Granted::content(old(&storage)) == Granted::content(&result.storage);

    machine read(placed: &Occupied) -> Slot;

    machine retire(placed: Occupied) -> Retired
    ensures
        Granted::content(old(&placed.storage)) == Granted::content(&result.storage);

    // The same view and retirement over the restated qualified extent: the
    // parameter domains are the exact indexed application, so a caller must
    // prove `Resident<SlotPlacement, Slot>` on the argument it passes.
    machine view(storage: &Extent in Granted & Resident<SlotPlacement, Slot>) -> Slot;

    machine retire_view(storage: Extent in Granted & Resident<SlotPlacement, Slot>) -> Retired
    ensures
        Granted::content(old(&storage)) == Granted::content(&result.storage);
}

// One allocation, established, read, retired, and recomposed with the tail.
machine exercise_resident(
    partition: &ExtentPartition,
    storage: &ResidentStorage,
    whole: Extent in Granted & Vacant,
    capacity: u64
) -> Extent
requires
    16 <= capacity;
    capacity == whole.length
reaches ExtentPartition, ResidentStorage
{
    let issued: Issued = allocate(
        partition,
        Bump { tail: whole, remaining: capacity },
        16,
    );
    let tail: Extent in Granted & Vacant = issued.strategy.tail;
    let vacant: Extent in Granted & Vacant = issued.allocation;
    let placed: Occupied = storage.place(vacant, Slot { value: 7 });
    let observed: Slot = storage.read(&placed);
    let retired: Retired = storage.retire(placed);
    let released: Extent in Granted & Vacant = retired.storage;
    let recomposed: Extent in Granted & Vacant = partition.merge(Split {
        taken: released,
        rest: tail,
    });
    recomposed as Extent
}

// The restated-local variant: `Occupied.storage` is restated through an
// exactly typed `let` and the qualified extent itself crosses the view and
// retirement calls.
machine exercise_resident_restated(
    partition: &ExtentPartition,
    storage: &ResidentStorage,
    whole: Extent in Granted & Vacant,
    capacity: u64
) -> Extent
requires
    16 <= capacity;
    capacity == whole.length
reaches ExtentPartition, ResidentStorage
{
    let issued: Issued = allocate(
        partition,
        Bump { tail: whole, remaining: capacity },
        16,
    );
    let tail: Extent in Granted & Vacant = issued.strategy.tail;
    let vacant: Extent in Granted & Vacant = issued.allocation;
    let placed: Occupied = storage.place(vacant, Slot { value: 7 });
    let resident: Extent in Granted & Resident<SlotPlacement, Slot> = placed.storage;
    let observed: Slot = storage.view(&resident);
    let retired: Retired = storage.retire_view(resident);
    let released: Extent in Granted & Vacant = retired.storage;
    let recomposed: Extent in Granted & Vacant = partition.merge(Split {
        taken: released,
        rest: tail,
    });
    recomposed as Extent
}

data RetiredSlot {
    case Empty;
    case Held(storage: Extent in Granted & Vacant);
}

machine retain_buffer(storage: Extent in Granted & Vacant) -> RetiredSlot {
    RetiredSlot::Held { storage: storage }
}

machine reset_retired(
    partition: &ExtentPartition,
    strategy: Bump,
    retired: RetiredSlot
) -> Bump
reaches ExtentPartition
{
    transition retired {
        RetiredSlot::Empty -> strategy
        RetiredSlot::Held { storage } -> restore(partition, strategy, storage)
    }

    state restore(
        partition: &ExtentPartition,
        strategy: Bump,
        storage: Extent in Granted & Vacant
    ) -> Bump {
        let whole: Extent in Granted & Vacant = partition.merge(Split {
            taken: storage,
            rest: strategy.tail,
        });
        let remaining: u64 = whole.length;
        Bump { tail: whole, remaining: remaining }
    }
}

machine exercise_retained(
    partition: &ExtentPartition,
    whole: Extent in Granted & Vacant,
    capacity: u64
) -> Extent
requires
    16 <= capacity;
    capacity == whole.length
reaches ExtentPartition
{
    let issued: Issued = allocate(partition, Bump { tail: whole, remaining: capacity }, 16);
    let retired: RetiredSlot = retain_buffer(issued.allocation);
    let restored: Bump = reset_retired(partition, issued.strategy, retired);
    let unchanged: Bump = reset_retired(partition, restored, RetiredSlot::Empty);
    unchanged.tail as Extent
}

data Main {}

machine Main::main(&mut self) {}
